From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wize-logic <wizelogic@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:32:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] recomp: lift the code a jump reaches outside its function
 into it

A function is lifted from its range, and a direct jump out of the range
was a tail call to the function starting at the target. When no function
starts there -- the middle of another body, the rest of this function past
a false function start, bytes no function covers -- the call went to a stub
that popped a return address and returned: the code never ran, and esp was
off by four, since a jump pushes nothing.

Halo 2276 had 270 such stubs inside lifted code, almost all reached by a
jcc or a jmp (407 and 140 sites, 3 calls). Its hs `and` evaluator at
0xE6600 is cut by a false start at 0xE66A0 (an immediate the disassembler
took for an address): `je 0xE66B6` returned through a stub, its epilogue
never ran, and a level script later crashed with esi in the script stack.
Most of the rest are alternate entries (tail_jump_alias bodies) whose
loops jump back into the part of their owner before them.

The translator now follows the code a function reaches: from its entry,
its switch arms and the continuations it jumps through, along
fall-through, direct jumps and switch arms. An address outside the
function's own instructions that no function starts at is decoded from
there and lifted into the function's body, as labels and gotos. That is
the x86's own behaviour (same stack, frame and flags), a loop that crosses
a false start stays a loop instead of a chain of host calls, and no
function is split, shortened or merged, so coalescence's evidence rules
are not involved. Decoding stops at a function start, which stays a tail
call. Bytes nothing reaches (a jump table decoded as code) are not
followed.

A switch whose arms lie past the function's end (a false start inside
it) has its table read with bounds: between the function starts with
structural evidence around the jump (prologue, call target, padding
boundary), and before the table when it follows the jump. The arms the
body holds become gotos wherever they sit in the table.

The lifter judges "outside" by the body's instructions instead of the
range: a target in the range with no instruction (it used to be a goto to
a missing label, dropped as dead code) is followed like any other.

Blocks are emitted in address order, so code lifted from below the entry
is reached by a goto to the entry, a block whose fall-through is not the
next block continues with a goto, and one that runs into a function start
tail-calls it. Functions that reach nothing outside themselves lift as
before. Halo: 314 functions gain 33,732 instructions (2,204 at most), and
the generated C grows 5.7%.

tests: test_interior_targets.py (a jcc and a jmp into another body, a
false start inside a loop with a switch, an alternate entry that jumps
back into its owner, a range that ends before its code, unreachable
bytes).
